The phases, verified:

```
CLICK on <button> inside <div> inside <body>:

1. CAPTURE (top → down):  window → document → body → div   (capture listeners fire)
2. TARGET:               button's own listeners fire
3. BUBBLE (bottom → up): div → body → document → window   (default listeners)

addEventListener('click', fn)              // BUBBLE leg (default)
addEventListener('click', fn, true)       // CAPTURE leg
addEventListener('click', fn, {capture: true, once: true, passive: true})
```

The two stops + the one cancel (the distinction interviewers test):
```js
event.stopPropagation();
// NO further NODES see it — div's handler already ran? Then body's won't.
// Does NOT stop the default action — the link still navigates.

event.stopImmediatePropagation();
// Also cancels REMAINING handlers on the SAME element

button.addEventListener('click', e => e.preventDefault());
// The browser's built-in action is cancelled: the <a> doesn't navigate,
// the form doesn't submit, the checkbox still toggles? No — checkbox
// default (checking) IS cancelled by preventDefault.
// Independent axis: stopPropagation = propagation; preventDefault = default.
```

The listener options (the modern details):
- once: true — auto-remove after first fire (one-time intro popups)
- passive: true — promises the handler won't preventDefault → the browser doesn't wait for it before scrolling (the scroll-performance flag every touch/wheel handler should carry)
- capture — intercept on the way DOWN: useful to run BEFORE a child's handlers can stopPropagation (analytics that must see every click, security wrappers)

The non-bubbling exceptions (the gotcha list): focus/blur don't bubble (use focusin/focusout), load doesn't bubble, mouseenter/leave don't (use mouseover/out). The delegation question (covered separately) inherits this constraint — delegate only bubbling events.
